WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:11.540
Part 3 You will hear two sociology students, called Tamara and Dev, discussing their research for a presentation on loneliness.

2
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.500
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 26.

3
00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:54.190
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 26.

4
00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.380
<v TAMARA> Shall we go through the notes we've made from our research into loneliness now, Dev?

5
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.540
<v DEV> OK, Tamara.

6
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.060
<v DEV> It's been a real eye-opener.

7
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:12.480
<v DEV> I had no idea that loneliness has been increasing steadily for the last 20 years.

8
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:13.580
<v TAMARA> I know.

9
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.540
<v TAMARA> And it's the same all over the world.

10
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.740
<v TAMARA> The downside of a modern lifestyle, I guess.

11
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.580
<v DEV> Did you come to any conclusions about what the reasons for the increase are?

12
00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:34.600
<v TAMARA> Well, I'd assumed it was mainly an issue for the elderly, but in fact it's something which affects young people just as much.

13
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.540
<v DEV> So nothing really to do with longer lifespans.

14
00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.960
<v DEV> What about social media?

15
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.080
<v DEV> In my case, far from making me feel isolated, it actually does the opposite.

16
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.520
<v TAMARA> It definitely does more good than harm.

17
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.060
<v TAMARA> I'd say loneliness has a lot to do with the way cities are designed.

18
00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:00.100
<v TAMARA> People living in high-rise flats with not much opportunity to speak to their neighbours.

19
00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:01.320
<v DEV> I think you're right.

20
00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:10.039
<v TAMARA> Another possible reason is that people are having fewer children and don't live in large extended family groups.

21
00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:14.540
<v DEV> But in this country, anyway, that all changed decades ago.

22
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.820
<v DEV> And yet loneliness is a more recent problem.

23
00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:19.580
<v TAMARA> I suppose so.

24
00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:29.960
<v TAMARA> A more plausible explanation is that people are having to move around for work and often end up living miles away from their family and friends.

25
00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:30.640
<v DEV> That's true.

26
00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:41.140
<v TAMARA> Looking at the studies on health risks and loneliness, there are claims that loneliness has as much impact as smoking 15 cigarettes a day.

27
00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.960
<v DEV> Or similar to the risks caused by obesity.

28
00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.800
<v DEV> But I'm not sure there's enough evidence for some of these claims.

29
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:57.740
<v TAMARA> Well, what about that one in Finland which showed that loneliness increased the risk of cancer by about 10%?

30
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.600
<v TAMARA> And those findings have been supported by other studies too.

31
00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.680
<v DEV> You're right about that one.

32
00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.720
<v DEV> I was actually thinking of the studies on dementia.

33
00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:15.180
<v DEV> Some found no association between loneliness and dementia and others found the opposite.

34
00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.740
<v TAMARA> Not exactly reliable then.

35
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:25.540
<v TAMARA> There's been a lot of research on cardiovascular disease and whether loneliness contributes to that.

36
00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:26.500
<v DEV> Yes.

37
00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:37.380
<v DEV> I read that it was hard to reach a judgement as the definition of loneliness varied quite a lot and the responses from participants were too subjective.

38
00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:43.120
<v DEV> But there's no doubt that loneliness contributes to a weakened immune system.

39
00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:44.620
<v TAMARA> Unquestionably.

40
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.940
<v TAMARA> The data on that is sound.

41
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.980
<v DEV> What did you think about the evolutionary theory of loneliness?

42
00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:58.920
<v TAMARA> Well, I thought the idea that loneliness evolved because it motivated people to be with other people quite convincing.

43
00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.860
<v TAMARA> Survival often depended on group cooperation.

44
00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:14.680
<v DEV> But I don't think there's enough evidence to claim that there must be a group of neurons in our brains which influence social behaviour by making us feel bad when we're alone.

45
00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:21.000
<v TAMARA> There are a few studies which support the theory but not conclusively enough.

46
00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:22.840
<v TAMARA> More evidence is needed.

47
00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:30.100
<v DEV> And anyway, this theory's not really useful when it comes to solving the problem of loneliness today.

48
00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:30.960
<v TAMARA> True.

49
00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:41.750
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 27 to 30.

50
00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:16.160
Now listen and answer questions 27 to 30.

51
00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:24.130
<v DEV> Should we look at the relationship between loneliness and mental health now?

52
00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:25.050
<v TAMARA> OK.

53
00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:33.730
<v TAMARA> So loneliness and depression are clearly related and that's been recognised by various governments around the world.

54
00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:39.990
<v TAMARA> But unlike depression, loneliness has no recognised clinical form.

55
00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:45.950
<v DEV> There's no available diagnosis or effective treatment and that's not likely to change.

56
00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:47.690
<v TAMARA> I don't think so either.

57
00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:54.610
<v TAMARA> I was thinking we should start our presentation with an example from our own experience.

58
00:05:55.690 --> 00:06:03.110
<v TAMARA> I'd like to talk about how lonely I was when I started university, being away from home for the first time and all that.

59
00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:04.110
<v DEV> Good idea.

60
00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:12.330
<v DEV> Everyone will be able to relate to that, although a lot of students were probably too embarrassed to admit to it.

61
00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:12.930
<v TAMARA> Yeah.

62
00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:20.130
<v TAMARA> We could discuss ways of dealing with loneliness as well, like just talking to strangers.

63
00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:30.150
<v DEV> Loads of studies have shown that interactions with shop assistants and bar staff make people feel more optimistic and relaxed.

64
00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:36.510
<v TAMARA> I don't know about that, but it must make people feel more connected with their community.

65
00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:37.150
<v DEV> True.

66
00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:43.470
<v DEV> Although you need to be a certain kind of person to be able to just strike up a conversation.

67
00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:44.570
<v TAMARA> Good point.

68
00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:53.130
<v TAMARA> We should say something about solitude and how being alone and being lonely aren't the same thing.

69
00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:59.350
<v TAMARA> It's strange the way some people can't stand being by themselves while others love it.

70
00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:11.090
<v DEV> Yeah, the research shows a certain amount of solitude is beneficial for well-being, which I appreciate, but being alone isn't something I actually like.

71
00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:14.530
<v DEV> I'd never choose to go on holiday alone, for example.

72
00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:15.710
<v TAMARA> Me neither.

73
00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:23.570
That is the end of Part 3.

74
00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:28.450
You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.
